# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction is taken: c1 is rcc's only back end, it writes x86-64
and aarch64 assembly itself, and the system cc preprocesses,
assembles and links. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and
=docs/llvm.md=, the design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made
in =docs/design-calls.md=. The chapter holds c1's own defects on both
targets; the second target's design is =docs/aarch64.md=.

** TODO x86-64: the comment on =register_decl()= sends every initializer through =establish()=

The comment in =src/c1-x86-64.cc= says "an initializer is moved into
the register by establish()". A constant initializer is not: it is
the constant row, written by =constant_into()=, =mov $c, %r12d=. The
aarch64 comment on the same function states both cases.

Fix: the comment names the constant row for a constant initializer,
as the aarch64 one does.

Check: the comment read against =register_decl()=.

** TODO =backend.md= 6.5 says a pinned register's value is exchanged by =xchg=

6.5 says a pinned register that holds a live value "is exchanged with
a free register (=xchg=, or a =mov= to a free one)". The generator
never writes =xchg=: =move()= and =vacate()= in =src/c1-x86-64.cc=
write =mov=, and no source file has the mnemonic.

Fix: 6.5 says the value moves to a free register by =mov=.

Check: =grep -n xchg docs/backend.md= prints nothing.

** TODO x86-64: =function()= resets every field of the state but =reserved=

The comment on =state_t= in =src/c1-x86-64.cc= says everything is
reset per function. =function()= resets the fields a line each and
leaves out =reserved=, the registers =take()= leaves for last. No
listing is wrong: the two functions that write the field,
=rvalue_avoiding()= and =rvalue()=, restore it before they return, so
it is 0 between functions.

Fix: =function()= resets it with the others.

Check: every x86-64 listing is byte-identical.

** TODO x86-64: the move of a shift's count into =cl= is written twice

=shift()= and =shift_on()= in =src/c1-x86-64.cc= each hold the same
eight lines: when the count is not in =rcx=, =vacate()= frees =rcx=,
and the count is copied there if it is a borrowed read and moved
otherwise.

Fix: one function, called from both.

Check: every x86-64 listing is byte-identical.

** TODO =c1.cc= includes =c1-common.hh= and uses nothing of it

=src/c1.cc= calls the two generators and throws =std::logic_error=
for an unknown target. It names nothing that =c1-common.hh= declares.

Fix: drop the include.

Check: =make= builds.

** TODO Four comments of the shared sources describe x86-64 alone

Each is in text both targets read, and each is true of one:

- =src/type.hh= on =size_of= and =align_of=, and =docs/shape.md=
  chapter 3, call the sizes and alignments "the x86-64 ABI's"; the
  aarch64 generator reads the same functions.
- =src/parse.cc= on =shift()= says the instruction reads the count's
  low bits "in cl".
- =src/c1-common.cc= on the register variable an lvalue names says
  the parser "declared the sixth an ordinary local"; on aarch64 it is
  the eleventh.

Fix: each says what holds for both targets: the sizes are LP64's, and
the count is the target's.

Check: no comment of =type.hh=, =parse.cc= or =c1-common.cc= names a
register or a number of one target alone.

** TODO A refusal of c1 names no place

c1's not-yets print "rcc: internal error: c1: not yet: a frame of 2
GiB or more" and exit with status 3. The message names no file, no
line and no function, since the tree carries no positions
(=shape.md= chapter 4). In a file of several functions the reader is
not told which one met the limit.

Fix: the message names the function being generated, which the
state holds.

Check: the five pins of c1's refusals in =tests/error= name =main=.

** TODO aarch64: a stack parameter at the frame's limit is refused

A parameter past the eighth is copied to its slot from above the
frame, at =[x29, #T + 8(i - 8)]= with =T= the frame's size
(=aarch64.md= 5.2). Past the load's reach the address is formed by
an =add= into =x16=, whose immediate is 12 bits shifted by 12 (5.3),
so it stops short of 16 MiB. The frame's own limit is 16 bytes under
16 MiB, and a frame there puts the eleventh parameter at 16 MiB:
~long f(long a, ..., long k) { char pad[16777096]; return k; }~, of
eleven parameters, stops the generator with "not yet: a stack
parameter 16 MiB or more above the frame record". With the array 16
bytes smaller it compiles.

Fix: for a displacement the =add= does not encode, the displacement
as a constant in =x16=, by the rule for constants, and =add x16,
x29, x16=. 5.2 loses its sentence on the limit.

Check: the program compiles and its listing assembles; run with the
stack limit raised, it returns its eleventh argument.

** TODO aarch64: a call takes 510 arguments past the eighth

The outgoing area of a call is opened by one =sub sp, sp, #A=, whose
immediate is 12 bits, and 8 bytes an argument rounded up to 16 make
the largest area 4080 bytes: 510 arguments past the eighth, 518 in
all. A call with more stops the generator with "not yet: an outgoing
area of 4096 bytes or more". x86-64 has no such limit, since it
pushes. Under 4096 bytes every store to the area is in reach at
every width, so the one limit covers the =sub= and the stores;
5.2.5.2 asks for 127 arguments in one function call.

Fix: the area opened and closed in two parts, as the frame is, and a
store past its instruction's reach (4095 bytes for =strb=, 8190 for
=strh=, 16380 for =str= of a =w= register, 32760 of an =x=) through
=x16= formed from =sp=. Chapter 8 loses its sentence on the limit.

Check: a call with 520 arguments to a function compiled by =clang=
returns the sum that tells the positions apart.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved. c1 writes none of the guard of G4, the round trip
of G8 and the laundering of G16, which draft 1 designed against an
optimizer, because nothing between the source and the instruction
reasons about the program.

** TODO No document gathers the implementation-defined choices

4p10 asks for "a document that defines all implementation-defined
and locale-specific characteristics and all extensions". rcc fixes
each choice per target in its code, and the documents record them
piecemeal: plain =char='s signedness on aarch64 in =docs/aarch64.md=,
the extent of =register= in =docs/cost.md= chapter 1, the narrowing
and the right shift of a negative value in G2 and G3.
=docs/guarantees.md= and =docs/rationale.md= say that no table
gathers them.

Fix: one table, per target, of the items of Annex J.3 that the
implemented subset reaches, each with its choice and the code or pin
that fixes it; the "Not in this list" of both documents points at it.

Check: every J.3 item the subset reaches has a row for each target,
and each row agrees with a pin or a probe.

** TODO =guarantees.md= names each guarantee's instruction for x86-64 alone

The page's opening says each entry gives "the instruction that
delivers it (=backend.md= chapter 9)", and the entries name x86-64's:
=movb=, =movsbl=, =lea=, =cqto=, a displacement off =rbp=, =call
f@PLT=. The aarch64 instructions are the table of =aarch64.md=
chapter 9, which the page does not name. A reader on aarch64 finds
another machine's instructions and no pointer to its own.

Fix: the opening says the instruction clauses are x86-64's and names
=aarch64.md= chapter 9 for the other target; or each clause names
both.

Check: a reader on aarch64 is sent to the table of its instructions.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
c1, its table written from the pins of =tests/cost/=. The findings of
the measurement (=docs/findings.md=) were facts about LLVM's code
generator and left with it; that page's chapter 8 says what became
of each. The entries below are rows the table lacks.

** TODO =cost.md= 4.1 does not state the metric of its table

4.1 sets the ratios of the LLVM pipeline's table beside those of
chapter 4's x86-64 table. The pipeline's table was taken in user time
alone; chapter 4's is user plus system. Each ratio is taken within
its own table, so the comparison stands, but 4.1 never says how its
table was timed and a reader takes the two as one method.

Fix: one clause in 4.1's opening that gives its metric.

Check: 4.1 states its metric, and it is the one its table was taken
with.

** TODO No row pins a multiply or an unsigned division by a power of two

c1 writes =x * 4= as =imull $4, %eax, %eax=, and an unsigned =x / 4=
and =x % 4= as =movl $4, %ecx; xorl %edx, %edx; divl %ecx=, the
remainder then moved from =edx= (=backend.md= 6.4). This is the
thesis's answer to finding 2 of =findings.md=, the selector's
strength reduction. But no cost program pins the three: =mul-three=
multiplies by 3 and =div-four= divides a signed value, so a change to
c1 that turned the three into a shift or a mask would show in no
row.

Fix: a cost program pinning =imull $4=, the =movl $4= into a
register and the =divl= of the unsigned division and remainder.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO No aarch64 pin shows a signed byte

Plain =char= is unsigned on aarch64 (=aarch64.md= chapter 11), so
every =char= of the cost corpus is loaded there with =ldrb= and
extended with =uxtb=: =cast='s =signed_()= and =narrow='s =f()= show
the unsigned forms on that target. No cost program declares a
=signed char=, so no aarch64 pin has =ldrsb= or =sxtb=, and a change
to the generator's signed byte load or extension would turn no row
red. The probes run the type; the pins do not show its listing.

Fix: a cost program with a =signed char= local, parameter and call
result, pinned for both targets.

Check: its aarch64 pin has =ldrsb= and =sxtb=, and perturbing either
in =c1-aarch64.cc= turns the row red.

** TODO The tl;dr says a register variable is never stored

=docs/cost.md='s tl;dr has a =register= variable "never stored", and
=README.md= has "a variable that is never stored". The variable has
no slot and no store at a write. But its register is stored to a
spill slot before a branch one of whose paths writes it while a read
of it is held (=backend.md= 6.7): =movq %rbx, -24(%rbp)= and =str
x19, [x29, #32]= in the two pins of =g13-read-across-branch=.

Fix: "has no slot of its own" in both places, with =backend.md= 6.7
named for the save.

Check: the sentence agrees with both pins of =g13-read-across-branch=.

* Tests

** TODO =tests/oracle --pin= says nothing of a pin that is the same

=tests/costs --pin= prints a line for every pin, new, changed or the
same. =tests/oracle --pin= prints one only for a new or a changed
pin, so a clean run adopts in silence and the two scripts read
differently.

Fix: the oracle prints "pinned <file> (same)" too.

Check: =tests/oracle --pin= on a clean tree prints one line for each
program.

** TODO The oracle's time limit cannot tell 124 from a timeout

GNU =timeout= exits 124 for a run it stopped and passes the program's
own status otherwise, so a program that exits 124 by itself is
recorded as =timeout=. And =timeout= sends SIGTERM, which a program
may ignore; =timeout -k= would follow with SIGKILL.

Fix: =timeout -k 1 10=, and a corpus rule that no program exits 124.

Check: a program that ignores SIGTERM ends its row.

** TODO A signal death records as 128 + n, like an exit with 128 + n

A program killed by signal n is recorded as 128 + n: =timeout= dies
of the child's signal, and the shell reports 128 + n. So the G9
program's SIGSEGV is the row's 139, and a program that exits 139 by
itself records the same.

Fix: show a status above 128 by its signal name, =SEGV= from =kill
-l=, in the table and in =expected=, and a corpus rule that no
program exits above 128.

Check: the =g9-null-load= row reads =SEGV= and passes.

** TODO A signal death leaves a core file

The oracle runs each program with the caller's core limit. Where it is
unlimited, every run of =g9-null-load= under rcc leaves a core file
where =core_pattern= says: in the tree's root where the pattern is
=core=, as =core.<pid>=, and in =/tmp= where it is
=/tmp/core.%e.%p.%s=, about 290 KB each; and =timeout= says "the
monitored command dumped core" in the log.

Fix: =ulimit -c 0= in the script before the runs.

Check: =make test= leaves no new =core.*= in the tree's root and none
in =/tmp=.

** TODO A G14 corpus program needs a non-constant condition

=for (;;);= hangs under =clang -O2= as well: C11 6.8.5p6 exempts a
loop whose controlling expression is constant from the
forward-progress assumption. The divergence G14 promises shows only
on a loop like =while (x);= with =x= a non-volatile object the loop
never writes.

Fix: write that program as the G14 entry, expected =timeout= under
rcc and 0 under clang.

Check: the oracle row.

** TODO The register rows pin only part of c1's register rules

The five programs that declare =register= pin the registers in
declaration order, the pushes with their unwind offsets, a register
parameter's move from its argument register, a read as an operand, a
copy before an instruction that writes, an assignment, a constant's
immediate, and G5's unwritten register. No program of either corpus
shows the rest of =backend.md= chapter 4, so a change to any of these
shows in no pin:

- a compound assignment or a =++= on the register in place, =addl $1,
  %r13d=;
- a narrow register variable: the extending store =movsbl %al,
  %ebx=, the re-extension after a step, and an unwritten one's
  extension at its declaration;
- the copy of a held read before a write, =i + i++=, and before the
  paths of =&&=, =||= and =?:= (G13);
- a register parameter past the sixth, loaded from its stack slot,
  and the order of the moves and the stores among mixed parameters;
- the epilogue's =leaq -8k(%rbp), %rsp= before the pops, which only a
  function with pushes and a =subq= writes;
- the sixth live variable in a slot, and a later block taking =rbx=
  again.

Fix: a cost program for each, with its pin.

Check: perturbing each rule in =c1-x86-64.cc= turns a row red.

** TODO =-o -= names a file, not standard output

=rcc -S -o -= writes a file called =-= in the working directory. Every
cc reads =-o -= as standard output for =-S=, and a reader of the
assembly expects it.

Fix: with =-S=, an output of =-= writes the text to standard output;
with =-c= or a link it stays a usage error.

Check: =rcc -S -o - x.c | head -1= prints the first line of the
assembly and leaves no file.

** TODO Two corpus programs describe the x86-64 listing only

The comments of =g7-ptr-equal= and =g11-misaligned= in =tests/corpus=
say what c1 writes by x86-64's names: "adjacent slots off rbp", and
"by one movl: x86 has one mov for every alignment". Each has an
aarch64 pin beside its x86-64 one, with slots off =x29= and an =ldr=,
and the comment is true of one pin of the two. =g5-register-undef=
and the three =g13-= programs name both targets' instructions.

Fix: each comment names both targets' instructions, as those four
do.

Check: no comment of =tests/corpus= names a register or an
instruction of one target alone.

** TODO =tests/slots= reads the clock of an idle core

The script reads =scaling_cur_freq= once, before the first timed
run, and computes the cycle column at that frequency. The core is
idle then. On a core with a frequency boost the reading is not the
clock a run gets: on the Zen 2 core with the boost on it read 3853
and 3593 MHz in two series, and =rr= came out at 0.84 and 0.78
cycles an iteration, under the one cycle its add takes. The script
prints the column all the same; the only sign is that the reading
after the last run differs, which its header mentions and its output
does not flag.

Fix: read the frequency while a run is in flight, or leave the cycle
columns out and say why when the two readings differ by more than a
percent.

Check: on an x86-64 core with its boost on, =make slots= prints =rr=
at about one cycle an iteration, or prints no cycle column and says
why.

** TODO The C sources of the tests do not follow =.clang-format=

=clang-format --dry-run tests/perf/*.c= flags all twelve: each keeps
a definition's return type on the line of its name, and =collatz*.c=
and =sort*.c= align their =#define= lines otherwise than the tree's
=.clang-format= does. The two files of =tests/perf/aarch64= follow
it, so they differ from their shared siblings by that layout.

The two corpora are laid out as the twelve are: =clang-format
--dry-run= prints 112 warnings over =tests/cost/*.c= and 24 over
=tests/corpus/*.c=, each program keeping a definition's return type
on the line of its name. The C sources of the tests follow
=.clang-format=, with one difference from the C++ sources: C is
indented by 8 spaces, C++ by 4. The file is written for C++ alone and
has no section for C.

Fix: a section for C in =.clang-format=, the C++ rules with an
indentation of 8; then every C source under =tests/= laid out by it.
No listing changes. A pin of =tests/error= changes in the line and
the column of its diagnostic and in nothing else, and a program that
is malformed on purpose is laid out by hand where the formatter
cannot read it.

Check: =clang-format --dry-run= over every =.c= and =.h= under
=tests/= prints nothing, every listing is byte-identical, and =make
test= passes.

** TODO The shared =matmul-tuned.c= says every loop ends on =!==

The header of =tests/perf/matmul-tuned.c= says "Every loop ends on
!=". The loops of the fill, the repetitions and the checksum end on
=<=; the three loops of the product end on =!==. The aarch64 file
says "Every loop of the product".

Fix: the shared header says it as the aarch64 one does.

Check: the header read against the loops.

** TODO The aarch64 =strings-tuned.c= draws a warning from =clang=

=clang -O2= warns at =copy()= of =tests/perf/aarch64/strings-tuned.c=,
"using the result of an assignment as a condition without
parentheses", on ~while (*d++ = *s++)~, and every =make bench= on
aarch64 writes the warning to =build/perf/log=. Writing the test as
~!= 0~ would silence it but is another program under rcc, which
compares where a comparison is written.

Fix: a second pair of parentheses, ~while ((*d++ = *s++))~.

Check: =make bench= on aarch64 writes no warning to the log, and the
listing of =copy()='s loop under rcc is unchanged.

* Parser

** TODO A warning recorded before an error is never printed

The parser records a warning in the unit, and the driver prints the
unit's warnings only after a successful parse. =error_t= ends the
parse at the first error, so the unit and its warnings are lost with
the exception. Thus =&k= on a sixth register variable reports the
error alone: the warning that put =k= in a slot was recorded at its
declaration, before the error, and is never printed.

Fix: the driver prints the warnings the parser recorded before the
error too. Since the unit is lost with the exception, the warnings
need a home the exception does not destroy, such as a list the
driver passes to the parser.

Check: =int f(void) { register int a, b, c, d, e, k; return *&k; }=
prints the warning at =k= and then the error at =&=, exit status 1.

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO A function is no value, and no call goes through a pointer

A pointer to a function is declared, stored, passed, returned and
compared, and the headers that declare one parse: of 58 headers of
the C library and of POSIX, 53 on aarch64 and 55 on x86-64. But a
function's name stands only as the callee of a call, "not yet:
function 'f' used as a value", and a call names its function, "not
yet: call through an expression". So =qsort=, =atexit=, =signal= and
=pthread_create= are declared and of no use: nothing a program has
can be passed to them.

Fix: a function is a value, its address, and a call is made through
any expression that is a pointer to a function: nothing in the subset
stands in the way of either. Both generators gain two rows, the
address of a function and the call through a pointer.

Check: a program that sorts with =qsort= and its own comparison, and
one that installs a handler with =signal= and raises its signal, run
on both targets, and a probe sets calls through pointers against
=clang='s.

** TODO On aarch64 =signal.h= stops at an array sized by a struct

With glibc's default feature set, aarch64's =signal.h= and
=sys/wait.h= reach =bits/procfs.h=, whose ~typedef elf_greg_t
elf_gregset_t[ELF_NGREG];~ has for its size ~sizeof (struct
user_regs_struct) / sizeof (elf_greg_t)~, and the parser stops with
"not yet: struct". =tests/corpus/header-signal.c= defines
=_POSIX_C_SOURCE=, under which the header leaves the file out; a
program that includes =signal.h= and defines nothing does not
compile on aarch64. On x86-64 both headers parse.

Fix: by the rule for a system header, until a struct has a size: a
typedef of an array whose size rcc cannot compute names a type rcc
declares and does not use.

Check: a program that includes =signal.h= alone compiles on both
targets, and an object of type =elf_gregset_t= is refused by name.

** TODO =stdatomic.h= does not parse

=clang='s =stdatomic.h= stops at ~typedef _Atomic(_Bool)
atomic_bool;~, "not yet: type specifier '_Atomic'". Three things
stand between the header and its end: the atomic type specifier
=_Atomic ( type-name )=; =_Bool=, which is not yet a type; and
~volatile atomic_flag *~ in a parameter. =volatile= and =_Atomic= as
qualifiers are taken after =typedef= only, where the typedef names a
type rcc declares and does not use, as =pthread_spinlock_t= is; a
qualified type written out anywhere else is not yet. Written out, each
occurrence would be a type of its own, so two declarations of one
function would conflict, and a pointer to =int= would not convert to
a pointer to =volatile int=, which is valid.

Fix: decide what =volatile= is in rcc. c1 writes every access it
meets, an unused one included (=shape.md= chapter 11), so the
qualifier may ask for nothing more and be dropped as =const= is; or
it waits for qualifiers in the type model. =_Atomic= and =_Bool= are
decided with it or after it.

Check: a program that includes =stdatomic.h= compiles, and the
documents say what a =volatile= object's access is.

** TODO A pointer to a type rcc does not honour takes no pointer to the type behind it

~typedef volatile int spin_t; void f(spin_t *); void g(int *p) {
f(p); }~ fails with "incompatible pointer types"; so does the same
program with ~typedef int word_t __attribute__ ((mode (word)));~. The
first is valid C, a conversion that adds a qualifier, and the error
calls it invalid. A typedef through a qualifier or a marking
attribute names a type of an identity of its own, which keeps nothing
of the type it was made from.

Fix: the refusal says "not yet" and names the typedef's words, where
one of the two pointees is such a type; or the type keeps what it was
made from.

Check: the first program is refused as not yet, or compiles.

** TODO A typedef through a marking attribute cannot be written twice

~typedef int w __attribute__ ((mode (word))); typedef int w
__attribute__ ((mode (word)));~ fails with "redefinition of typedef
'w' with another type". A typedef may be declared again as the same
type, and these two are. Each declaration through an attribute rcc
does not honour makes a type of an identity of its own, so the
second is never the first.

A typedef through =volatile= or =_Atomic= written twice fails
the same way.

Fix: a typedef declared again through the same marking attribute, or
the same qualifier, on the same type takes the earlier declaration's
type.

Check: the program above compiles; with =mode (byte)= in the second
it still fails.

** TODO A marking attribute on a later declaration does not reach an earlier call

~int f(void); int g(void) { return f(); } int f(void) __attribute__
((weak));~ compiles. The attribute marks the symbol from where it is
written, and the call parsed before it was accepted. GNU C lets a
later declaration add an attribute, and the use rcc cannot honour has
then been taken.

Fix: decide whether an attribute rcc does not honour, met after a
use of the symbol, is refused where it is written.

Check: the program above is refused, at the attribute or at the
call.

** TODO The other target is preprocessed with this machine's headers

=rcc -target x86-64= on an aarch64 machine runs =clang -E
--target=x86_64-linux-gnu=, which searches =/usr/include=: the
aarch64 glibc's headers, read with x86-64's predefined macros. For
=stdio.h= the two texts are the same here, so the pin of
=header-stdio= for the other target is honest; a header that differs
by architecture, =bits/= and =asm/= above all, would give the other
target this machine's declarations.

Fix: say in =shape.md= chapter 8 that a program with system headers
is compiled for the machine's own target, and refuse =-target= for
another machine when the program includes one; or take a sysroot.

Check: the documents and the driver agree on what =-target= does to
=#include <...>=.

** TODO A definition that is =_Noreturn inline= is compiled

~_Noreturn inline void f(void) { for (;;) { } }~ compiles as an
external definition, with =.globl f=. ~inline _Noreturn~ on the same
definition is "not yet: 'inline' on a function definition", as
=inline= alone is. =external_declaration()= asks whether the first
function specifier written is =inline=, and here it is =_Noreturn=.

Fix: the test is whether =inline= was written among the specifiers,
which =declaration_specifiers()= records since the =static inline=
definitions of the headers.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with the definition above, pinned
"not yet: 'inline' on a function definition".

** TODO A block's declaration that begins with an attribute is read as an expression

~int main(void) { __attribute__ ((unused)) int x = 1; return x; }~
fails with "expected expression" at the attribute. At file scope the
same declaration is read. =is_declaration_start()= does not take the
attribute keyword, so a block reads the tokens as a statement.

Fix: =is_declaration_start()= takes the attribute keyword; the
attribute is then read among the specifiers, dropped or not yet on a
local by its kind.

Check: the program above compiles and returns 1; with ~__attribute__
((aligned (16)))~ it is refused as not yet, by the attribute's name.

** TODO A qualifier inside a parameter's array brackets is an error

=regex.h= stops at ~regmatch_t __pmatch[__restrict __nmatch]~ of
=regexec=, "expected expression": a parameter's array declarator may
hold =static= and type qualifiers before its size, which say
something of the pointer the parameter is adjusted to. The size there
names an earlier parameter, which the entry on a parameter's array
size is about.

Fix: the qualifiers and =static= inside a parameter's brackets are
read and dropped, =restrict= checked as it is on a pointer.

Check: with the entry on a parameter's array size fixed too, a
program that includes =regex.h= compiles.

** TODO A function type keeps no marking attribute of its parameters

A marking attribute on a parameter marks the function a declaration
declares, so that a call is refused. On ~typedef int f_t(int x
__attribute__ ((mode (word))));~ and on ~int (*p)(int x __attribute__
((mode (word))));~ nothing is recorded: the mark is the symbol's, a
function type has none, and the attribute is lost. No program shows
it yet: a function declared through such a typedef is "not yet:
function 'f' declared through a typedef", and a call through a
pointer is not yet.

Fix: the function type carries the marking, so that a call through a
pointer to it and a function declared through the typedef are
refused as a call of a marked function is.

Check: a call through the pointer above is refused by the
attribute's name.

** TODO A typedef for =void= is not the empty parameter list

~typedef void V; int f(V);~ fails with "'void' must be the only
parameter". C takes a single unnamed parameter of type =void= as "no
parameters", whatever spells the type. =parameter_type_list()= asks
for the keyword =void= followed by =)=, and a typedef name is not the
keyword.

Fix: the test is on the parameter's type after its specifiers, not on
the token.

Check: ~typedef void V; int f(V) { return 0; }~ compiles to the
listing of ~int f(void)~.

** TODO A prototype's parameter does not hide a typedef name

~typedef int T; int f(int T, T x);~ is accepted. In C the first
parameter declares an object named =T= in the prototype's scope, so
the second =T= names that object and =T x= is an error. The parser
declares a definition's parameters after the whole declarator and a
prototype's never, so the typedef stays visible. The entry on a
parameter's array size naming an earlier parameter is the same gap.

Fix: the scope opened at the parameter list, each parameter declared
as its declarator completes.

Check: the program above fails at the second =T=.

** TODO Two declarations of an array, one without its size, conflict

~extern int a[]; extern int a[3];~ fails with "conflicting types for
'a'". C finds the two types compatible, since one size is absent, and
the object has the composite type, =int [3]=. Type equality in the
tree is structural, with no notion of compatibility or of a composite
type.

Fix: at a redeclaration, an array of unknown size is compatible with
one of known size and equal element type, and the symbol takes the
sized type.

Check: the program above compiles; ~extern int a[2]; extern int
a[3];~ still fails.

** TODO A typedef followed by a function body draws an error about a semicolon

~typedef int f(void) { return 0; }~ fails with "expected ';' after
top level declarator" at the brace. The program is wrong, and the
message does not say how: a typedef cannot define a function.

Fix: a message of its own where a typedef's function declarator
meets a brace.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with its pin.

** TODO The comments on typedef and extern cite no paragraph

The comments written with =typedef= and =extern= state their rules
without the standard's numbers, since no copy was at hand to verify
them against: a label's name space, the declaration of a =for=, the
storage class of a parameter, a typedef redeclared as the same type,
a typedef name as a type specifier, =extern= on a function, an
=extern= object without an initializer, a typedef's initializer. Two
existing citations were widened unverified: 6.2.7p2 to objects of
file scope, and 6.5.2p2 to "as an object or a function".

Fix: each rule cited by its paragraph in N3220, read from the text.

Check: every citation in the comments of =src/parse.cc= that this
entry names is found at its number in N3220.

** TODO A tag first written in a parameter list outlives the prototype

~void f(struct S *); struct S { int x; }; void f(struct S *p) { }~ is
accepted. In C a tag first declared in a parameter list has the
prototype's scope, so the first =struct S= is not the second, and the
two declarations of =f= have conflicting types. The parser declares
such a tag in the scope around the declarator. The entry on a
prototype's parameter that does not hide a typedef name is the same
missing scope.

Fix: with the scope opened at the parameter list, a tag declared
there is declared in it; a definition's parameter tags are its body's.

Check: the program above fails with "conflicting types for 'f'".

** TODO A cast to a struct says not yet

~(struct S) x~ fails with "not yet: struct". The cast is not waiting
for a slice: C takes only =void= or a scalar type in a cast, so "cast
to a type that is not a scalar", which the tree has, is the
diagnosis. A cast to an enum or to a floating type is valid C, and
"not yet" is right for those.

Fix: =cast()= refuses a struct or a union as not a scalar, before it
asks whether the type is one rcc does not use yet.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with ~(struct S) x~, pinned "cast
to a type that is not a scalar".

** TODO A parameter's array size cannot name an earlier parameter

=int f(int n, int a[n])= fails with "'n' undeclared". Before the
evaluator, any size but one integer token was a not-yet; the size is
now parsed as an expression, and the name in it is looked up. The
parser declares a definition's parameters only after the whole
declarator, in =function_definition=, and a prototype's never, so no
parameter is in scope inside the list. The program is valid C: =n='s
scope begins just after its declarator (6.2.1p7), and a definition's
parameter list has block scope (6.2.1p4). The size changes nothing:
6.7.7.4p6 adjusts the parameter to a pointer, and at prototype scope
6.7.7.3p5 treats a size that is not an integer constant expression as
=*=.

Fix: a scope opened at the parameter list, each parameter declared as
its declarator completes, which a definition keeps as its body's
scope; a parameter's size that is not an integer constant expression
taken as =*=. In a definition this skips the evaluation of the size
that 6.7.7.3p5 asks for, which only a size with a side effect shows.

Check: =int f(int n, int a[n]) { return a[0]; }= compiles to the
listing of =int f(int n, int *a)=; =int g(int n) { int a[n]; }= is
still the variable-length not-yet.

** TODO A =(void *) 0= arm makes =?:= a =void *=

=c ? (void *) 0 : p= with =p= an =int *= has type =void *= in the
tree, so =*(c ? (void *) 0 : p)= fails with "indirection through a
pointer to void". 6.5.16p7 gives the result the type of the other
operand when one operand is a null pointer constant, and =(void *) 0=
is one (6.3.2.3p3); the result is an =int *=. =common_pointer()=
asks whether an operand is a null pointer constant only when it is
not a pointer. Two pointers of different types thus meet at =void *=
whenever one is a =void *=, which is p7's rule for a pointer to void
that is not a null pointer constant.

Fix: in =common_pointer()=, a null pointer constant takes the other
operand's type before the rule for =void *= applies.

Check: =int f(int c, int *p) { return *(c ? (void *) 0 : p); }=
compiles; =c ? v : p= with =v= a =void *= variable is still =void *=.

** TODO The operand of =!= is not promoted

=!E= is defined as =(0==E)= (6.5.4.3p5), and the equality operator
promotes a =char= operand to =int=. The parser builds =!c= on the
unpromoted =char=, and c1 compares it at the =char='s width, =cmpb
$0=. The result is the same, 0 or 1, but the tree does not say what
the standard says, and the listing shows a byte compare where the
operation is on an =int=.

Fix: the parser inserts the promotion cast on the operand of =!=; c1
then writes =cmpl=.

Check: the listing of =!c= on a =char= is the load =movsbl= and
=cmpl $0=.

** TODO A conditional with two void arms is refused

~a < b ? f() : g();~ with =f= and =g= returning =void= fails with
"not yet: conditional with void operands". It is valid: 6.5.16p3
lists "both operands have void type" among the pairs the second and
third operands may be, and p6 says "If both operands have void type,
the result has void type." =conditional_expression()= converts each
arm to a value and finds their common type, and a void arm has
neither. The strict form of glibc's =assert= is this expression,
~((e) ? (void) (0) : __assert_fail(...))~.

Fix: with two void arms the parser converts neither and types the
node =void=. Such a node stands only where a value is dropped, an
expression statement, the left of a comma, a cast to =void=, an arm
of another conditional, and c1 takes a conditional's arms for their
effect there; whether either generator needs more is found with the
fix.

Check: ~void f(int *p) { *p = 1; }~, ~void g(int *p) { *p = 2; }~
and ~c ? f(&x) : g(&x);~ compile under both targets and store the
arm the condition selects.

** TODO The comment on a local's declaration says 2 GiB

The comment on =init_declarator()= says "A local of 2 GiB or more is
refused here". The test under it is against the target's frame limit,
which is 16 bytes under 2 GiB on x86-64 and 16 bytes under 16 MiB on
aarch64: =char a[20000000];= as a local compiles with =-target
x86-64= and fails with =-target aarch64=, "variable 'a' is too large
for a local", status 1.

Fix: the comment says a local over the target's largest frame is
refused, and names no number.

Check: the comment read against =src/target.hh=.

** TODO =convert_to()= is not the one place a conversion enters the tree

The comment on =convert_to()= in =src/parse.cc= calls it "The one
place a conversion enters the tree". =decayed()= makes a cast node of
its own, for an array's decay, which =src/ast.hh= calls "a cast too".
The cast the source writes, made in =cast()=, the
comment does allow for.

Fix: the comment says every conversion but an array's decay.

Check: =grep -n 'cast_t {' src/parse.cc= prints the sites the
comments name.

** TODO A subscript's errors speak of addition

~p[q]~ with two pointers passes the subscript's own check and fails
in =addition()= with "cannot add two pointers"; ~v[0]~ on a =void *=
fails there with "arithmetic on a pointer to void". Both are at the
=[=, and neither names the subscript. 6.5.3.2p1 states the constraint
for the subscript itself: one operand a pointer to a complete object
type, the other an integer. No program of =tests/error= draws either
through a subscript.

Fix: =subscript()= refuses two pointers, and a pointer to =void=,
with messages about the subscript, before it calls =addition()=.

Check: two programs of =tests/error=, ~int f(int *p, int *q) { return
p[q]; }~ and ~int f(void *v) { return v[0]; }~, pinned with the new
messages.

** TODO An error at the end of the file names a line the file does not have

A function body the file ends inside is reported at the end-of-file
token: =tests/error/unclosed-function-body.c= has four lines, and
the error is at 5:1. =clang= reports the same program at the end of
the last token, 4:14. A reader, or an editor following the position,
is sent past the text.

Fix: the end-of-file token takes the position just after the last
token; or an error at end of file is reported at the token before
it.

Check: the pins of =unclosed-function-body= and =unclosed-block=
name the last line of their programs.

** TODO A void initializer is reported at the ===

~int x = g();~ with =g= returning =void= fails with "void expression
used as a value" at the ===, not at the call: =init_declarator()=
hands the === token to =assignment_conversion()=, which is the right
place for a conversion's error and the wrong one for an operand that
has no value.

Fix: the check for a value reports at the initializer's first token.

Check: the pin of =tests/error/void-value= names the column of =g=.

* Lexer

** TODO A binary or a bit-precise constant is an invalid integer constant

=0b101= and =1wb= are C23's (6.4.4.2). The parser has a "not yet:
binary constant" and a "not yet: bit-precise integer constant" for
them, and an error for a suffix it does not know, but none of the
three is reached: the lexer's =number()= stops at the =b= and at the
=wb= and refuses each first, "invalid integer constant '0b101'". A
valid constant is thus called invalid, and the parser's three
branches are dead.

Fix: the lexer takes the binary prefix with its digits and the =wb=
suffix as part of the number, so the parser's not-yets say what is
missing; or the lexer says "not yet" itself and the parser's
branches go.

Check: two programs of =tests/error=, pinned "not yet: binary
constant" and "not yet: bit-precise integer constant".

** TODO A digit separator is a missing quote

~1'000~ is C23's (6.4.4.2). The parser's "not yet: digit separator"
is never reached: rcc runs =clang -E= with no =-std=, clang's default
dialect has no separator, so clang warns of an open character
constant, and the lexer, which ends the number at the quote, reports
"missing terminating ' character".

Fix: decide the dialect rcc preprocesses in. With =-std=c23= passed
to =clang -E= and the quote taken by =number()=, the not-yet is
reached; without, the parser's branch goes.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with ~1'000~ and its pin.

** TODO A floating constant draws one of two errors, and neither says not yet

=.5= is refused with "floating constant not supported"; =1.5= and
=1e3= with "invalid integer constant '1.5'" and "'1e3'". The types
=float= and =double= are "not yet: floating type", and =shape.md=
chapter 11 lists floating point among what is not yet. A valid
constant is called an invalid integer, and the three spellings of
one thing read as two different errors. =clang='s =tgmath.h= stops
there, at a =0.F= inside the body of a =static= function: the lexer
reads the body before the parser skips it.

Fix: =number()= and =punctuator()= say "not yet: floating constant"
for a fraction or an exponent.

Check: =.5=, =1.5= and =1e3= each pinned "not yet: floating
constant".

** TODO A character outside ASCII in a character constant is called multi-character

='é'= written in the source reaches the lexer as the two bytes of its
UTF-8 encoding, and the lexer reports "not yet: multi-character
constant". The source has one character. The same character in a
string literal compiles, as its two bytes, with no diagnostic; the
escape =é= is "not yet: universal character name" in both.

Fix: a byte above 127 in a character constant is refused by its own
name; whether a string literal takes such bytes in silence is decided
with it and recorded in the table of implementation-defined choices.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with ='é'= and its pin.

** TODO The keyword table of =src/lex.cc= does not follow =.clang-format=

=clang-format --dry-run src/lex.cc= prints 23 warnings, all on the
table =keywords[]=: the table is written two entries a line in
aligned columns, and the formatter lays it out one entry a line. No
other C++ source draws a warning.

Fix: the table one entry a line, as the formatter has it.

Check: =clang-format --dry-run src/*.cc src/*.hh= prints nothing.
